{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"cf31d801-4234-4bd2-bed9-2d7a3e178a24","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Start with a block on a frictionless incline. The surface rises at angle theta; our diagram uses thirty degrees.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Block on a frictionless incline"},{"op":"figure","id":"incline","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-3.7,3.7],"y":[-2.6,3],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"slope","from":[-3,-1.732],"to":[3,1.732]},{"kind":"segment","from":[-3,-1.732],"to":[0,-1.732],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"angle","at":[-3,-1.732],"from":[0,-1.732],"to":[3,1.732],"r":0.2,"label":"θ"},{"kind":"polyline","points":[[-0.866,-0.5],[0.866,0.5],[0.366,1.366],[-1.366,0.366]],"closed":true},{"kind":"point","id":"center","at":[-0.25,0.433]}]},{"op":"equation","id":"angle_value","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\theta=30^\\circ"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"cf31d801-4234-4bd2-bed9-2d7a3e178a24","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Only two forces act: weight vertically downward and the normal force perpendicular to the surface. Neither force points along the incline.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"forces","on":"incline","marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"weight","from":{"at":"center"},"to":[-0.25,-1.867],"arrow":true,"label":"mg"},{"kind":"segment","id":"normal","from":{"at":"center"},"to":[-1.246,2.158],"arrow":true,"label":"N"}]},{"op":"text","id":"force_note","anchor":{"el":"angle_value","side":"below","gap":0.12},"content":"Weight: vertical. Normal: perpendicular."}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.warning","request_id":"cf31d801-4234-4bd2-bed9-2d7a3e178a24","code":"step_repaired","step_hint":"s3"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"cf31d801-4234-4bd2-bed9-2d7a3e178a24","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Resolve weight into downhill and into-surface components. These dashed arrows replace weight in calculations; they are not additional forces.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"components","on":"incline","marks":[{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"center"},"to":[-1.246,-0.142],"arrow":true,"stroke":"dashed","label":"mg sin θ"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"center"},"to":[0.746,-1.292],"arrow":true,"stroke":"dashed","label":"mg cos θ"},{"kind":"segment","from":[-1.246,-0.142],"to":{"on":"weight","t":1},"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"segment","from":[0.746,-1.292],"to":{"on":"weight","t":1},"stroke":"dashed"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"balance","anchor":{"el":"force_note","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"N-mg\\cos\\theta=0"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"cf31d801-4234-4bd2-bed9-2d7a3e178a24","step":{"id":"s4","script":"The normal balances only the perpendicular component. Downhill, Newton's second law gives acceleration g sine theta: half of g for thirty degrees.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"motion","on":"incline","marks":[{"kind":"segment","from":[-1.7,1.1],"to":[-3,0.35],"arrow":true,"label":"a"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"newton","anchor":{"el":"balance","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"ma=mg\\sin\\theta"},{"op":"equation","id":"result","anchor":{"el":"newton","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"a=g\\sin30^\\circ=\\frac{g}{2}\\approx4.9\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
